# Realtime Conventions (Socket.io)

One Socket.io namespace/room per Watchly room, addressed by the room's `uid`. This file is
the "don't break these invariants" summary — full event list belongs alongside the server
code, not duplicated in multiple places.

- The **Socket.io server is authoritative** for playback state and room membership — it's
  not a thin relay in front of some other source of truth. Client-sent playback events
  (play/pause/seek) are requests, not commands — validate the sender is the current admin
  (or has been granted playback control) against the server's own in-memory record before
  applying and broadcasting.
- Playback sync: broadcast the current state on every control event, using a server-side
  "source of truth" timestamp rather than trusting client clocks. Clients reconcile drift
  against periodic heartbeats; small drift is left alone, only hard-seek past a small
  threshold (~1–1.5s) — don't correct on every tick regardless of drift, it makes playback
  visibly stutter.
- Room state (playback position, presence, admin/participant roles, playback-control
  grants) lives directly in the Socket.io server's memory — not Postgres. Durable state
  (accounts, room configs, friendships) goes through Supabase Postgres — never the reverse,
  and never write live playback position there.
- Admin-only actions (kick, mute, ban, admin transfer, granting playback control) are
  re-checked server-side against the room's own record of who the admin is on every call —
  hiding a button client-side is not access control.
- Bans: check a banned user's ID server-side before accepting a socket connection to that
  room, on both the join-by-UID and join-by-invite paths.
- Chat is ephemeral and real-time only — broadcast to the room, never persisted to
  Postgres. Cleared when the room closes or a user disconnects; no chat history on rejoin.
- Admin disconnect without transfer: define a fallback server-side (e.g. auto-promote the
  longest-present participant, or auto-close the room after a timeout) so a room doesn't
  get stuck with no admin.
- Server restart loses in-memory room state — acceptable for v1 given ephemeral chat and
  transient live state, but don't build features that assume state survives a restart.
- WebRTC signaling (offer/answer/ICE candidates for peer-to-peer voice) is relayed through
  the same Socket.io connection — don't introduce a second signaling channel.
